226
00:15:42,385 --> 00:15:48,154
which under Providence we ought to achieve
reforms in the civil service must go on.

227
00:15:48,154 --> 00:15:54,308
But the changes should be real and genuine
, not perfunctory or prompted by a zeal

228
00:15:54,308 --> 00:16:00,462
in behalf of any party simply because it
happens to be in power as a member

229
00:16:00,462 --> 00:16:05,077
of Congress I voted
and spoke in favor of the present law

230
00:16:05,077 --> 00:16:10,462
and I shall attempt its enforcement
in the spirit in which it was enacted

231
00:16:10,462 --> 00:16:11,462
the purpose.

232
00:16:11,462 --> 00:16:17,615
In view was to secure the most efficient
service of the best men who would accept

233
00:16:17,615 --> 00:16:19,923
appointment under
the government retaining faithful

234
00:16:19,923 --> 00:16:25,308
and devoted public servants in office, but
shielding none under the authority of any

235
00:16:25,308 --> 00:16:29,923
rule or custom who are inefficient
incompetent or unworthy the best interests

236
00:16:29,923 --> 00:16:34,923
of the country demand this and the people
heartily approve the law wherever

237
00:16:34,923 --> 00:16:39,923
and whenever it has been thus administrate
d Congress should give prompt attention

238
00:16:39,923 --> 00:16:46,462
to the restoration of our American merchan
t Marine once the pride of the seas in all

239
00:16:46,462 --> 00:16:51,462
the great ocean highways of commerce
to my mind few more important subjects.

240
00:16:51,462 --> 00:16:53,769
So imperatively demand
its intelligent consideration.

241
00:16:53,769 --> 00:16:58,000
The United States has progressed
with marvelous rapidity in every field

242
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:03,385
of enterprise and endeavor until we have
become foremost in nearly all the great

243
00:17:03,385 --> 00:17:06,077
lines of inland trade commerce
and industry.

244
00:17:06,077 --> 00:17:11,462
Yet while this is true our American mercha
nt Marine has been steadily declining

245
00:17:11,462 --> 00:17:15,692
until it is now lower
both in the percentage of tonnage

246
00:17:15,692 --> 00:17:21,462
and the number of vessels employed than
it was prior to the civil war commendable

247
00:17:21,462 --> 00:17:24,154
progress has been made of late years.

248
00:17:24,154 --> 00:17:26,846
In the upbuilding of the American Navy.

249
00:17:26,846 --> 00:17:32,231
But we must supplement these efforts
by providing as a proper consort for it,

250
00:17:32,231 --> 00:17:37,231
a merchant Marine amply sufficient for our
own carrying trade to foreign countries.

251
00:17:37,231 --> 00:17:41,462
The question is one that appeals
both to our business necessities

252
00:17:41,462 --> 00:17:44,538
and the patriotic aspirations
of a great people.

253
00:17:44,538 --> 00:17:48,000
It has been
the policy of the United States.

254
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,231
Since the foundation of the government
to cultivate relations of peace

255
00:17:52,231 --> 00:17:58,000
and amity with all the nations of the
world, and this accords with my conception

256
00:17:58,000 --> 00:17:59,154
of our duty.

257
00:17:59,154 --> 00:18:03,769
Now we have cherished the policy of
non-interference with affairs of foreign

258
00:18:03,769 --> 00:18:05,692
governments wisely
inaugurated by Washington.

259
00:18:05,692 --> 00:18:10,692
Keeping ourselves free from entanglement
either as allies or foes content to leave

260
00:18:10,692 --> 00:18:14,538
undisturbed with them the settlement
of their own domestic concerns.

261
00:18:14,538 --> 00:18:19,538
It will be our aim to pursue a firm
and dignified foreign policy

262
00:18:19,538 --> 00:18:23,769
which shall be just impartial
ever watchful of our national honor

263
00:18:23,769 --> 00:18:28,769
and always insisting upon the enforcement
of the lawful rights of American citizens

264
00:18:28,769 --> 00:18:29,769
everywhere.

265
00:18:29,769 --> 00:18:35,154
Our diplomacy should seek nothing more
and accept nothing less than is due us.

266
00:18:35,154 --> 00:18:37,462
We want no wars of conquest.

267
00:18:37,462 --> 00:18:40,538
We must avoid
the temptation of territorial aggression.

268
00:18:40,538 --> 00:18:44,769
War should never be entered
upon until every agency of peace,

269
00:18:44,769 --> 00:18:49,000
has failed peace is preferable
to war in almost every contingency.

270
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:54,385
Arbitration is the true method of settleme
nt of international as well as local

271
00:18:54,385 --> 00:18:55,538
or individual differences.

272
00:18:55,538 --> 00:19:00,923
It was recognized as the best means of adj
ustment of differences between employers

273
00:19:00,923 --> 00:19:04,000
and employees by the forty-ninth
Congress in 1886

274
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,615
and its application was extended to our
diplomatic relations by the unanimous

275
00:19:08,615 --> 00:19:13,231
concurrence of the Senate and House
of the fifty-first Congress in 1890,

276
00:19:13,231 --> 00:19:17,846
the latter resolution was accepted
as the basis of negotiations with us

277
00:19:17,846 --> 00:19:23,231
by the British House of commons in 1893
and upon our invitation a treaty

278
00:19:23,231 --> 00:19:28,231
of arbitration between the United States
and great Britain was signed at Washington

279
00:19:28,231 --> 00:19:31,308
and transmitted to the Senate
for its ratification.

280
00:19:31,308 --> 00:19:32,308
In January.

281
00:19:32,308 --> 00:19:36,923
Last since this treaty is clearly
the result of our own initiative,

282
00:19:36,923 --> 00:19:42,692
since it has been recognized as the leadin
g feature of our foreign policy throughout

283
00:19:42,692 --> 00:19:43,846
our entire history.

284
00:19:43,846 --> 00:19:48,462
The adjustment of difficulties by judicial
methods rather than force of arms

285
00:19:48,462 --> 00:19:51,154
and since it presents to the world.

286
00:19:51,154 --> 00:19:53,846
The glorious example of reason and peace,

287
00:19:53,846 --> 00:19:59,231
not passion and war controlling the relati
ons between two of the greatest nations

288
00:19:59,231 --> 00:20:03,462
in the world, an example
certain to be followed by others.

289
00:20:03,462 --> 00:20:09,231
I respectfully urge the early action of
the Senate thereon not merely as a matter

290
00:20:09,231 --> 00:20:10,231
of policy.

291
00:20:10,231 --> 00:20:15,615
But as a duty to mankind the importance
and moral influence of the ratification

292
00:20:15,615 --> 00:20:21,385
of such a treaty can hardly be overestimat
ed in the cause of advancing civilization.

293
00:20:21,385 --> 00:20:27,154
It may well engage the best thought of
the statesmen and people of every country

294
00:20:27,154 --> 00:20:32,923
and I cannot but consider it fortunate
that it was reserved to the United States

295
00:20:32,923 --> 00:20:39,462
to have the leadership in so grand a work
it has been the uniform practice of each

296
00:20:39,462 --> 00:20:44,462
President to avoid as far as possible
the convening of Congress in extraordinary

297
00:20:44,462 --> 00:20:45,462
session.

298
00:20:45,462 --> 00:20:51,615
It is an example which under ordinary circ
umstances and in the absence of a public

299
00:20:51,615 --> 00:20:53,538
necessity is to be commended.

300
00:20:53,538 --> 00:20:59,308
But a failure to convene the representativ
es of the people in Congress in extra

301
00:20:59,308 --> 00:21:05,077
session when, it involves neglect of a pub
lic duty places the responsibility of such

302
00:21:05,077 --> 00:21:09,308
neglect upon the Executive himself
the condition of the public Treasury.

303
00:21:09,308 --> 00:21:11,231
As has been indicated demands.

304
00:21:11,231 --> 00:21:13,154
The immediate consideration of Congress.

305
00:21:13,154 --> 00:21:17,385
It alone has the power
to provide revenues for the government,

306
00:21:17,385 --> 00:21:20,077
not to convene
it under such circumstances.

307
00:21:20,077 --> 00:21:25,462
I can view in no other sense
than the neglect of a plain duty.

308
00:21:25,462 --> 00:21:31,615
I do not sympathize with the sentiment tha
t Congress in session is dangerous to our

309
00:21:31,615 --> 00:21:32,769
general business interests.

310
00:21:32,769 --> 00:21:38,154
Its members are the agents of the people,
and their presence at the seat

311
00:21:38,154 --> 00:21:42,769
of government in the execution
of the sovereign will should not operate

312
00:21:42,769 --> 00:21:49,308
as an injury, but a benefit there could be
no better time to put the government upon

313
00:21:49,308 --> 00:21:52,385
a sound financial
and economic basis than now.

314
00:21:52,385 --> 00:21:56,615
The people have only recently
voted that this should be done.

315
00:21:56,615 --> 00:22:02,000
And nothing is more binding upon the
agents of their will than the obligation

316
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:03,154
of immediate action.

317
00:22:03,154 --> 00:22:09,308
It has always seemed to me that the postpo
nement of the meeting of Congress until

318
00:22:09,308 --> 00:22:10,846
more than a year.

319
00:22:10,846 --> 00:22:13,154
After it has been chosen deprived.

320
00:22:13,154 --> 00:22:17,000
Congress, too often of the inspiration
of the popular will

321
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,692
and the country
of the corresponding benefits.

322
00:22:19,692 --> 00:22:25,077
It is evident, therefore, that to postpone
action in the presence of so great

323
00:22:25,077 --> 00:22:29,308
a necessity would be unwise
on the part of the Executive

324
00:22:29,308 --> 00:22:32,385
because unjust to the interests
of the people.

325
00:22:32,385 --> 00:22:38,154
Our action now will be freer from mere par
tisan consideration than if the question

326
00:22:38,154 --> 00:22:42,385
of tariff revision was postponed
until the regular session of Congress,

327
00:22:42,385 --> 00:22:45,846
we are nearly two
years from a congressional election,

328
00:22:45,846 --> 00:22:50,846
and politics cannot so greatly distract
us as if such contest was immediately

329
00:22:50,846 --> 00:22:51,846
pending.

330
00:22:51,846 --> 00:22:57,231
We can approach the problem calmly and pat
riotically without fearing its effect upon

331
00:22:57,231 --> 00:22:58,385
an early election.

332
00:22:58,385 --> 00:23:03,385
Our fellow-citizens who may disagree with
us upon the character of this legislation

333
00:23:03,385 --> 00:23:06,077
prefer to have the question settled now,

334
00:23:06,077 --> 00:23:11,077
even against their preconceived views and
perhaps settled so reasonably as I trust,

335
00:23:11,077 --> 00:23:16,462
and believe it will be as to insure
great permanence than to have further

336
00:23:16,462 --> 00:23:21,077
uncertainty menacing the vast and varied
business interests of the United States

337
00:23:21,077 --> 00:23:22,077
again.

338
00:23:22,077 --> 00:23:27,462
Whatever action Congress may take will be
given a fair opportunity for trial before

339
00:23:27,462 --> 00:23:34,000
the people are called to pass judgment upo
n it and this I consider a great essential

340
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:39,000
to the rightful and lasting settlement
of the question in view of these

341
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,615
considerations, I shall deem it my
duty as President to convene Congress

342
00:23:43,615 --> 00:23:45,538
in extraordinary session on Monday.

343
00:23:45,538 --> 00:23:47,846
The 15th day of march 1897.

344
00:23:47,846 --> 00:23:52,846
In conclusion I congratulate the country
upon the fraternal spirit of the people

345
00:23:52,846 --> 00:23:55,538
and the manifestations
of good will everywhere

346
00:23:55,538 --> 00:23:59,385
so apparent the recent election
not only most fortunately demonstrated

347
00:23:59,385 --> 00:24:02,077
the obliteration
of sectional or geographical lines,

348
00:24:02,077 --> 00:24:07,846
but to some extent also the prejudices whi
ch for years have distracted our councils

349
00:24:07,846 --> 00:24:09,769
and marred our true greatness.

350
00:24:09,769 --> 00:24:12,846
As a nation, the triumph of the people.

351
00:24:12,846 --> 00:24:15,154
Whose verdict is carried into effect.

352
00:24:15,154 --> 00:24:20,154
Today is not the triumph of one section,
nor wholly of one party,

353
00:24:20,154 --> 00:24:26,308
but of all sections and all the people
the North and the South no longer divide

354
00:24:26,308 --> 00:24:31,308
on the old lines, but upon principles
and policies and in this fact,

355
00:24:31,308 --> 00:24:35,923
surely every lover of the country
can find cause for true felicitation.

356
00:24:35,923 --> 00:24:39,000
Let us rejoice
in and cultivate this spirit.

357
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:45,154
It is ennobling and will be both a gain
and a blessing to our beloved country,

358
00:24:45,154 --> 00:24:51,692
it will be my constant aim to do nothing,
and permit nothing to be done that will

359
00:24:51,692 --> 00:24:55,538
arrest or disturb this growing
sentiment of unity and cooperation.

360
00:24:55,538 --> 00:24:59,000
This revival of esteem
and affiliation which now animates

361
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,462
so many thousands
in both the old antagonistic sections.

362
00:25:02,462 --> 00:25:07,077
But I shall cheerfully do everything
possible to promote and increase it.

363
00:25:07,077 --> 00:25:12,462
Let me again repeat the words of the oath
administered by the Chief justice

364
00:25:12,462 --> 00:25:15,923
which in their respective spheres,
so far as applicable.

365
00:25:15,923 --> 00:25:20,923
I would have all my countrymen, observe,
I will faithfully execute the office

366
00:25:20,923 --> 00:25:26,692
of President of the United States and will
to the best of my ability preserve,

367
00:25:26,692 --> 00:25:30,154
protect, and defend
the Constitution of the United States.

368
00:25:30,154 --> 00:25:36,308
This is the obligation I have reverently
taken before the Lord most high to keep it

369
00:25:36,308 --> 00:25:41,692
will be my single purpose my constant
prayer and I shall confidently rely upon

370
00:25:41,692 --> 00:25:47,077
the forbearance and assistance of all
the people in the discharge of my solemn

371
00:25:47,077 --> 00:25:48,077
responsibilities.
